~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
[EXPLAIN] The four tests
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Rucker et al. (2007) define y-randomization as permuting the potencies and repeating the WHOLE
  model-building         procedure, because the risk of chance correlation comes mainly from
  selecting a few descriptors out of many. They         also propose a second comparison, with
  descriptors replaced by random ones (pseudodescriptors), because real         descriptors are
  intercorrelated and shuffled potencies alone give lower chance scores.
  Each test is run on the stacking alone ("architecture", all 2048 bits) and on the procedure that
  produces the         screening model ("procedure": the out-of-fold permutation-importance bit
  selection of step 04, redone in every         training set, then the stacking). Scores are
  5-fold cross-validated R2: tree ensembles fit even random         potencies almost perfectly, so
  the R2 of the fit would not discriminate. A test passes when the permutation         p-value is
  <= 0.01.
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
